      "then keeps the eve that holds the old patch, because its version still matches the pin, and\r",
      "the new patch does not apply over the old one. When that apply fails and eve already\r",
      "carries an `__oe` symbol, the applier runs `npm pack eve@<pin>`. npm reads the tarball from its cache,\r",
      "or from the registry when the cache does not hold it, and checks its integrity hash. The applier\r",
      "copies back every eve file whose bytes differ from the tarball, prints each path, then applies the\r",
      "patch again. The restore also discards any other change to those files, whatever made it. A failed\r",
      "fetch, unpack or copy leaves eve unchanged. A failed rename (a file that Windows holds open) can\r",
      "leave eve partly restored, and the refusal says so. The fetch writes only to npm's cache and a\r",
      "temporary directory. When the applier cannot restore eve, it exits 1 and prints the recovery\r",
      "commands. They delete `node_modules/eve` with a `node -e` call, which runs in every shell, and then\r",
      "run the install of the package manager that ran the applier. When the apply fails and eve carries\r",
      "no `__oe` symbol, something else changed eve, and the applier refuses as before.\r",
      "`scripts/eve-patch-upgrade-packed-test.ts` drives both routes against a packed core and a real eve\r",
      "that holds the newest released OE patch for the current pin (on the 0.54.3 pin, the verbatim 0.1.4\r",
      "patch). When no release shipped a patch for the pin, no app can hold an older OE patch on that eve\r",
      "version. The leg then drives neither route: it says so and passes, so the restore route and the\r",
      "refusal route have no test on that pin.\r",
      "\r",
      "🔴 **Under pnpm and bun the applier never writes a target in place, and this is not optional.** Both\r",
      "import a package's files from a content store by **hardlink**, so one inode is shared with every\r",
      "project on the machine that installed that eve. A `writeFileSync(path, …)` on a target truncates that\r",
      "inode rather than replacing the directory entry. Left alone, one consumer running the applier would\r",
